# SPDX-FileCopyrightText: 2026 Frederic Ruget <fred@atlant.is> (GitHub: @douzebis)
#
# SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT

# Guided walk through grpconf2026/anomalies.pb — every encoding anomaly
# prototext-core can report, ordered from most to least interesting for a
# security audience.
#
# Invocation:
#   protolens --descriptor-set $PROTOTEXT_WKT_SET \
#       --type google.protobuf.FileDescriptorProto \
#       grpconf2026/anomalies.pb \
#       --script grpconf2026/anomalies.script

title: Protobuf encoding anomalies

steps:
  - text: |
      This document contains one example of every encoding anomaly
      prototext can detect — ordered from most to least interesting.

      Each example lives in its own submessage, under a heading that
      stays visible however far the document is folded.

      👋 Press space to hand navigation here, then -> to step through.
    fold: ["/ 1"]

  # ── 1. Shadowed scalar ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  - text: |
      1. Shadowed scalar.

      The schema declares `name` singular — it should appear at most once.
      The wire carries it twice.

      Standard decoders apply last-write-wins: the first value is silently
      overwritten and never reaches the application.

      👋 Unfold to see both occurrences.
    fold: ["/ 1"]
    node: /2
    select_line: true
    advance_when:
      - visible: /2/1

  - text: |
      The second occurrence: `repeated_singular`.
      It is the value the application sees.

      The first occurrence: `shadowed_scalar`.
      It is invisible to every standard SDK and to protoc --decode.

      This is a data exfiltration vector: content hidden at the wire
      level, below the application layer.

      👋 Press `w` on the shadowed line to inspect the bytes.
    fold: ["/ 1", "/2 Z"]
    node: /2/1
    select_line: true
    advance_when:
      - wire: /2/1

  - text: |
      The bytes are there. Nothing malformed — the value round-trips
      faithfully. It is simply never delivered to the application.

      👋 Press `w` to close, then -> to step.
    fold: ["/ 1", "/2 Z"]
    node: /2/1
    wire_line: /2/1
    select_line: true
    advance_when:
      - not:
        - wire: /2/1

  # ── 2. OverHanging Bytes (ohb) ────────────────────────────────────────────

  - text: |
      2. OverHanging Bytes — legal but non-canonical.

      A varint may carry trailing 0x80 padding bytes. Every parser in
      the world accepts them and decodes the same value. But no canonical
      writer produces them.

      2.a. A TAG varint padded to 3 bytes (1 would suffice).
      The amber color signals non-canonical encoding.

      👋 Press `w` to compare the bytes of both lines.
    fold: ["/ 1", "/4 Z"]
    node: /4/1
    select_line: true
    wire_lines:
      from: /4/1
      to: /4/2
    advance_when:
      - not:
        - wire: /4/1

  - text: |
      2.b. A LENGTH PREFIX padded to 4 bytes.
      Same principle, same color, different position in the encoding.

      👋 Press `w` to compare.
    fold: ["/ 1", "/6 Z"]
    node: /6/1
    select_line: true
    wire_lines:
      from: /6/1
      to: /6/2
    advance_when:
      - not:
        - wire: /6/1

  - text: |
      2.c. A VALUE varint padded to 5 bytes.
      The integer 5 — one byte of information — written in five.

      Deliberate padding is a fingerprint. It can also encode a covert
      channel: five bytes for a value that needs one, leaving four bytes
      of padding whose content is ignored by all standard parsers.

      👋 Press `w` to compare.
    fold: ["/ 1", "/8 1", "/8/1 Z", "/8/2 Z"]
    node: /8/1/2
    wire_line: /8/1/2
    select_line: true
    advance_when:
      - not:
        - wire: /8/1/2

  # ── 3. Unknown enum / unknown fields ─────────────────────────────────────

  - text: |
      3.a. An enum value this schema has no name for.

      The wire carries the number 99. The schema knows no name for it.
      prototext shows the number as-is and marks it ENUM_UNKNOWN.

      Nothing is lost — the value round-trips exactly.

      👋 Press -> to step.
    fold: ["/ 1", "/10 1", "/10/1 Z", "/10/2 Z"]
    node: /10/1/2
    wire_line: /10/1/2
    select_line: true

  - text: |
      3.b. Fields the schema does not declare at all.

      Each is rendered by its wire type — varint, fixed64, fixed32, bytes
      — which is all the tag bytes themselves can tell us.

      This is forward compatibility: a newer sender, an older schema.
      The data is preserved; the names are just not known yet.

      👋 Press -> to step.
    fold: ["/ 1", "/12 Z"]
    node: /12/1
    select_line: true
    wire_lines:
      from: /12/1
      to: /12/4

  # ── 4. Truncated message ──────────────────────────────────────────────────

  - text: |
      4. A truncated message.

      The length prefix promises more bytes than the buffer contains.
      protoc --decode fails entirely on this input.

      prototext decodes as far as possible, annotates the boundary with
      TRUNCATED_MESSAGE / TRUNCATED_BYTES, and continues.

      In log forensics, truncation is evidence of a process killed
      mid-write — as we saw in bob/logfile.

      👋 Press -> to step.
    fold: ["/ 1", "/14 Z"]
    node: /14/1
    wire_line: /14/1
    select_line: true

  # ── 5. Schema / wire disagreement ────────────────────────────────────────

  - text: |
      5.a. Type mismatch: schema says string, wire says varint.

      The wire type in the tag is the ground truth. prototext shows
      what the bytes actually are and marks TYPE_MISMATCH.

      👋 Press -> to step.
    fold: ["/ 1", "/16 Z"]
    node: /16/1
    wire_line: /16/1
    select_line: true

  - text: |
      5.b. Declared a string, but the payload is not valid UTF-8.

      The bytes are shown as-is with the ill-formed sequence marked.

      👋 Press -> to step.
    fold: ["/ 1", "/18 Z"]
    node: /18/1
    wire_line: /18/1
    select_line: true

  - text: |
      5.c. Declared packed int32, but the payload does not decode.

      The last byte promises continuation that never comes.
      prototext keeps the record whole rather than guessing.

      👋 Press -> to step.
    fold: ["/ 1", "/20 Z"]
    node: /20/1/1
    wire_line: /20/1/1
    select_line: true

  # ── 6. Round-trip survivors ───────────────────────────────────────────────

  - text: |
      6.a. -1 written in five bytes instead of the specified ten.

      The spec says a negative int32 travels as a 10-byte sign-extended
      varint. Some producers truncate it to five. The value is identical;
      a re-encode would write the longer form.

      👋 Press `w` to compare.
    fold: ["/ 1", "/22 1", "/22/1 Z", "/22/2 Z"]
    node: /22/1/2
    wire_line: /22/1/2
    select_line: true
    advance_when:
      - not:
        - wire: /22/1/2

  - text: |
      6.b. A non-canonical NaN.

      IEEE 754 has 2^52 distinct NaN bit patterns. Protobuf text has one
      spelling: `nan`. A re-encode silently picks the canonical one.
      prototext preserves the original bits.

      👋 Press -> to step.
    fold: ["/ 1", "/24 1"]
    node: /24/1/1
    wire_line: /24/1/1
    select_line: true

  # ── 7. Packed run ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  - text: |
      7. A packed repeated field.

      Three text lines share one tag and one length prefix on the wire —
      one wire record. Press `w` to see the single run of bytes.

      The middle element is a 0 padded to 3 bytes (ohb).
      The last is a -1 truncated to 5 bytes (neg).
      The canonical run below is actually longer: sign-extending -1
      costs more than the padding saves.

      👋 Press -> to step.
    fold: ["/ 1", "/26 1", "/26/1 Z"]
    node: /26/1/1
    wire_node: /26/1/1
    select_line: true

  # ── 8. Malformed bytes ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  - text: |
      8. Malformed wire bytes.

      From here on the bytes are not merely unusual — they are
      unreadable. Each one ends the scan of its enclosing region.
      The length-prefix boundary limits the damage to one submessage.

      8.a. A varint with no terminating byte. The continuation bit
      of the last byte promises another one; there is none.

      👋 Press -> to step through the remaining cases.
    fold: ["/ 1", "/28 Z"]
    node: /28/1
    wire_line: /28/1
    select_line: true

  - text: |
      8.b. A length prefix that is itself an unterminated varint.
    fold: ["/ 1", "/30 Z"]
    node: /30/1
    wire_line: /30/1
    select_line: true

  - text: |
      8.c. A 64-bit field with only 3 bytes behind it.
    fold: ["/ 1", "/32 Z"]
    node: /32/1
    wire_line: /32/1
    select_line: true

  - text: |
      8.d. A 32-bit field with only 2 bytes behind it.
    fold: ["/ 1", "/34 Z"]
    node: /34/1
    wire_line: /34/1
    select_line: true

  - text: |
      8.e. Wire type 6 — no such wire type exists.
      The three type bits in the tag are wrong; nothing after them
      can be located.
    fold: ["/ 1", "/36 Z"]
    node: /36/1
    wire_line: /36/1
    select_line: true

  - text: |
      8.f. Field number 0 — out of range (field numbers start at 1).
      The wire type is fine; only the number beside it is marked.
    fold: ["/ 1", "/38 Z"]
    node: /38/1
    wire_line: /38/1
    select_line: true

  # ── 9. Groups ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  - text: |
      9. Groups — proto2 legacy, included for completeness.

      A group is opened by a START_GROUP tag and closed by an END_GROUP
      tag with the same field number, with no length prefix. Nothing in
      this schema declares groups, so they arrive as undeclared fields.

      9.a. Padded closing tag (etag_ohb), then the same group closed
      canonically.
    fold: ["/ 1", "/40 1", "/40/1 Z", "/40/2 Z"]
    node: /40/1
    wire_node: /40/1
    select_line: true

  - text: |
      9.b. Opened as field 100, closed as field 101 — END_MISMATCH.
    fold: ["/ 1", "/42 1", "/42/1 Z"]
    node: /42/1
    wire_node: /42/1
    select_line: true

  - text: |
      9.c. Closed with field number 2^29 — out of range.
    fold: ["/ 1", "/44 1", "/44/1 Z"]
    node: /44/1
    wire_node: /44/1
    select_line: true

  - text: |
      9.d. A group that is never closed — OPEN_GROUP.
    fold: ["/ 1", "/46 1", "/46/1 Z"]
    node: /46/1
    wire_node: /46/1
    select_line: true

  - text: |
      9.e. An END_GROUP tag that closes nothing — INVALID_GROUP_END.

      That is the complete vocabulary.

      👋 Press space to return navigation to the main pane and explore
      freely. The script does not constrain anything.
    fold: ["/ 1", "/48 Z"]
    node: /48/1
    wire_line: /48/1
    select_line: true
